NNAAMMEE
       fstab - static information about the filesystems

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The file ffssttaabb contains descriptive information about the filesystems
       the system can mount. ffssttaabb is only read by programs, and not written;
       it is the duty of the system administrator to properly create and
       maintain this file. The order of records in ffssttaabb is important because
       ffsscckk(8), mmoouunntt(8), and uummoouunntt(8) sequentially iterate through ffssttaabb
       doing their thing.

       Each filesystem is described on a separate line. Fields on each line
       are separated by tabs or spaces. Lines starting with '#' are comments.
       Blank lines are ignored.

       The following is a typical example of an ffssttaabb entry:

           LABEL=t-home2   /home      ext4    defaults,auto_da_alloc      0  2

   TThhee ffiirrsstt ffiieelldd ((_f_s___s_p_e_c))..
       This field describes the block special device, remote filesystem or
       filesystem image for loop device to be mounted or swap file or swap
       partition to be enabled.

       For ordinary mounts, it will hold (a link to) a block special device
       node (as created by mmkknnoodd(2)) for the device to be mounted, like
       _/_d_e_v_/_c_d_r_o_m or _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b_7. For NFS mounts, this field is _<_h_o_s_t_>_:_<_d_i_r_>,
       e.g., _k_n_u_t_h_._a_e_b_._n_l_:_/. For filesystems with no storage, any string can
       be used, and will show up in ddff(1) output, for example. Typical usage
       is _p_r_o_c for pprrooccffss; _m_e_m, _n_o_n_e, or _t_m_p_f_s for ttmmppffss. Other special
       filesystems, like uuddeevv and ssyyssffss, are typically not listed in ffssttaabb.

       LABEL=<label> or UUID=<uuid> may be given instead of a device name.
       This is the recommended method, as device names are often a coincidence
       of hardware detection order, and can change when other disks are added
       or removed. For example, 'LABEL=Boot' or
       'UUID=3e6be9de-8139-11d1-9106-a43f08d823a6'. (Use a filesystem-specific
       tool like ee22llaabbeell(8), xxffss__aaddmmiinn(8), or ffaattllaabbeell(8) to set LABELs on
       filesystems).

       It’s also possible to use PPAARRTTUUUUIIDD== and PPAARRTTLLAABBEELL==. These partitions
       identifiers are supported for example for GUID Partition Table (GPT).

       See mmoouunntt(8), bbllkkiidd(8) or llssbbllkk(8) for more details about device
       identifiers.

       Note that mmoouunntt(8) uses UUIDs as strings. The string representation of
       the UUID should be based on lower case characters. But when specifying
       the volume ID of FAT or NTFS file systems upper case characters are
       used (e.g UUID="A40D-85E7" or UUID="61DB7756DB7779B3").

   TThhee sseeccoonndd ffiieelldd ((_f_s___f_i_l_e))..
       This field describes the mount point (target) for the filesystem. For
       swap partitions, this field should be specified as `none'. If the name
       of the mount point contains spaces or tabs these can be escaped as
       `\040' and '\011' respectively.

   TThhee tthhiirrdd ffiieelldd ((_f_s___v_f_s_t_y_p_e))..
       This field describes the type of the filesystem. Linux supports many
       filesystem types: ext4, xfs, btrfs, f2fs, vfat, ntfs, hfsplus, tmpfs,
       sysfs, proc, iso9660, udf, squashfs, nfs, cifs, and many more. For more
       details, see mmoouunntt(8).

       An entry _s_w_a_p denotes a file or partition to be used for swapping, cf.
       sswwaappoonn(8). An entry _n_o_n_e is useful for bind or move mounts.

       More than one type may be specified in a comma-separated list.

       mmoouunntt(8) and uummoouunntt(8) support filesystem _s_u_b_t_y_p_e_s. The subtype is
       defined by '.subtype' suffix. For example 'fuse.sshfs'. It’s
       recommended to use subtype notation rather than add any prefix to the
       first fstab field (for example 'sshfs#example.com' is deprecated).

   TThhee ffoouurrtthh ffiieelldd ((_f_s___m_n_t_o_p_s))..
       This field describes the mount options associated with the filesystem.

       It is formatted as a comma-separated list of options. It contains at
       least the type of mount (rroo or rrww), plus any additional options
       appropriate to the filesystem type (including performance-tuning
       options). For details, see mmoouunntt(8) or sswwaappoonn(8).

       Basic filesystem-independent options are:

       ddeeffaauullttss
           use default options: rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, and async.

       nnooaauuttoo
           do not mount when mmoouunntt --aa is given (e.g., at boot time)

       uusseerr
           allow a user to mount

       oowwnneerr
           allow device owner to mount

       ccoommmmeenntt
           or xx--<<nnaammee>> for use by fstab-maintaining programs

       nnooffaaiill
           do not report errors for this device if it does not exist.

   TThhee ffiifftthh ffiieelldd ((_f_s___f_r_e_q))..
       This field is used by dduummpp(8) to determine which filesystems need to be
       dumped. Defaults to zero (don’t dump) if not present.

   TThhee ssiixxtthh ffiieelldd ((_f_s___p_a_s_s_n_o))..
       This field is used by ffsscckk(8) to determine the order in which
       filesystem checks are done at boot time. The root filesystem should be
       specified with a _f_s___p_a_s_s_n_o of 1. Other filesystems should have a
       _f_s___p_a_s_s_n_o of 2. Filesystems within a drive will be checked
       sequentially, but filesystems on different drives will be checked at
       the same time to utilize parallelism available in the hardware.
       Defaults to zero (don’t check the filesystem) if not present.

